message.content:
You are building the 3D scene for STOP/PROVE, a hackathon project (neatHack 2026, judged partly on how impressive the demo looks). Work fast and autonomously; you have about 2.5 hours. Do not ask questions; make good decisions.

## The project
STOP/PROVE is an AI agent that commissions PLC control logic for an industrial conveyor. It is given a buggy control program (with hidden seeded faults: missing E-stop reset, a timing race at the pick position, unsupervised stuck sensor, auto-restart after the guard door closes), tests it in a simulator, proposes restricted patches, proves safety rules with a model checker, and submits a repaired program. The web viewer replays the agent's run and the simulated scenarios in an interactive 3D digital twin of the conveyor cell built in three.js. Your job: the 3D digital twin. It must look stunning (high-poly, PBR, cinematic) and be faithful to the simulation data.

## Repo and ownership
- Repo root (git worktree): `C:\Users\omerj\Downloads\neathack-agent\.claude\worktrees\neathack-hackathon-e0846b`. Always use absolute paths. Never `cd` to `C:\Users\omerj\Downloads\neathack-agent` itself.
- Web app: `web/` (Vite 5 + TypeScript + three@0.170.0, already installed; run `npm install <pkg>` inside `web/` only if you truly need something more, pinned to an exact version).
- READ FIRST: `web/src/types.ts` — the data contract (Frame, Trace, PLANT constants). The plant geometry there is authoritative: belt x from 0 (infeed) to 4.0 m (discharge end), belt speed 0.5 m/s, box length 0.4 m, photo-eye PE_IN at x=0.3, PE_END at x=3.7, a box whose centre passes x=3.9 falls off the end.
- YOU OWN ONLY: `web/src/scene/**` and `web/public/assets/**`. Another engineer is simultaneously building the UI (`web/src/ui/**`, `web/src/sim/**`, `web/src/main.ts`, `web/index.html`, styles). Do not edit their files. For your own testing create `web/scene-test.html` + `web/src/scene/dev.ts` (a standalone page that mounts your stage and animates a mock trace) — you own those two files too.

## Git rules (important)
- Commit your own work in small, meaningful commits as you go (aim for 8–12 commits, e.g. "Add beveled aluminium conveyor frame", "Add photo-eye sensors with live beams", ...). Stage ONLY your paths explicitly: `git add web/src/scene web/public/assets web/scene-test.html` — never `git add -A` or `git add .`.
- Commit message: a short imperative subject line, optional body, and end with a blank line then `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`.
- Do NOT push, do NOT run git stash/reset/checkout/rebase/merge. If a commit fails because `.git/index.lock` exists (another engineer committing at the same moment), wait a few seconds and retry.
- Keep assets small: total `web/public/assets` under ~20 MB (use 1k textures, 1k/2k HDRI). Asset files must be CC0.

## What to build: `web/src/scene/`
Export from `web/src/scene/index.ts` this API (the UI engineer codes against it — keep it exactly):

```ts
import type { Frame, Violation } from '../types';
export type CellId = 'A' | 'B';
export type PanelControl = 'START' | 'STOP' | 'RESET' | 'ESTOP' | 'GUARD';
export interface StageOptions { quality?: 'high' | 'low' }
export interface Stage {
  /** Show one cell, or two side-by-side twin cells ("A" = BASELINE, "B" = REPAIRED) with floating labels. */
  setLayout(layout: 'single' | 'twin', labels?: { A: string; B: string }): void;
  /** Apply a simulation frame to a cell. Called every animation frame during replay with interpolated time; must be cheap. prev is the previous frame (for edges). */
  applyFrame(cell: CellId, frame: Frame, prev?: Frame): void;
  /** Visual alarm for a safety violation (red flash/pulse at the relevant spot + floating tag). */
  flashViolation(cell: CellId, v: Violation): void;
  /** Camera presets with smooth easing. 'auto' = slow cinematic orbit. */
  focus(target: 'overview' | 'infeed' | 'pick' | 'panel' | 'guard' | 'auto'): void;
  /** Called when the user clicks a control on the operator panel (or the guard door) in cell A. */
  onControl(cb: (c: PanelControl) => void): void;
  /** Whether panel clicks are enabled (true in interactive "Play" mode). */
  setInteractive(on: boolean): void;
  resize(): void;
  dispose(): void;
}
export function createStage(container: HTMLElement, opts?: StageOptions): Stage;
```
The stage owns its renderer/canvas inside `container`, runs its own requestAnimationFrame render loop (continuous animation for belt texture scroll, motor spin, lamp glow, box falling, picker motion, idle camera drift), and listens to container resize (ResizeObserver).

### Mapping simulation data to visuals (must be faithful)
- `frame.out.MOTOR` = 1: belt texture scrolls toward +x at 0.5 m/s, rollers rotate, motor fan/shaft spins; 0: everything still.
- `frame.boxes`: for each box with state 'belt' place it on the belt at its `x` (centre, metres along the belt). Boxes appear when they show up in the frame, disappear when missing. 'picked': play a pick animation (an overhead gantry pick head with suction cups descends at the pick position x=3.7, lifts the box up and away) then hide. 'dropped': the box tips over the discharge end and falls to the floor with a short physical-looking tumble, stays on the floor lying at an angle, with a red pulsing ring.
- `frame.in.PE_IN` / `frame.in.PE_END`: photo-eye beams across the belt at x=0.3 and x=3.7: thin glowing beam (green when clear, red/orange when blocked) plus an LED on the sensor housing. If `frame.stuck?.PE_IN` (or PE_END) is defined, show the sensor as faulty: magenta flicker + a small "STUCK" tag.
- `frame.in.START/STOP/RESET`: the corresponding panel button is visibly pressed (depressed + brighter glow). `frame.in.ESTOP`=1: the mushroom E-stop is pushed in. `frame.in.GUARD_CLOSED`=0: the interlocked guard door is swung open (animate the hinge).
- Stack light (signal tower) segments: `LAMP_GREEN`, `LAMP_AMBER`, `LAMP_RED` — emissive, with bloom when on, dim glass when off. Also the illuminated START button glows when MOTOR=1.
- flashViolation: red light pulse + a CSS2D tag with the rule id and message near the relevant spot (pick end for "S6"/"DROP", panel for S1/S3/S5, guard door for S2/S4, infeed for S7), fading after ~3 s.

### Visual design (this is what wins — go big)
- Industrial cell, high-poly and detailed: beveled aluminium T-slot extrusion side rails (ExtrudeGeometry with a real T-slot profile + bevel), support legs with cross braces and levelling feet, ~24 steel rollers (instanced, 48+ radial segments), dark rubber belt with a subtle textured pattern (CanvasTexture or Poly Haven texture) and animated UV, drive unit at the discharge end (motor with cooling fins via LatheGeometry/instancing, gearbox, coupling guard), yellow end-stop bumper detail, sensor brackets + housings + reflectors, cable trays/cables (TubeGeometry), safety fence of mesh panels (alphaMap mesh texture or instanced bars) with yellow posts and one interlocked hinged guard door with an interlock switch, operator control panel on a pedestal (enclosure, buttons: green START, red STOP, blue RESET, red mushroom E-STOP on yellow collar, labels), stack light tower, overhead pick gantry with suction head, cardboard boxes (rounded box geometry with cardboard PBR + tape strip + label decal via CanvasTexture), floor: polished concrete PBR with yellow/black hazard floor markings around the cell.
- CC0 assets from Poly Haven are encouraged (use the API: `https://api.polyhaven.com/files/<asset_id>` lists download URLs; download with curl into `web/public/assets/`). Good picks: an indoor HDRI for reflections (e.g. a factory/warehouse/studio HDRI, 1k), concrete floor texture (1k), maybe metal textures. Use the HDRI as `scene.environment` (PMREM) and keep the background a dark moody gradient/fog so the cell pops like a product shot.
- Lighting/render: ACESFilmic tone mapping, sRGB output, soft shadows (PCFSoft, tuned shadow camera), a key spot light + rim lights, subtle volumetric-looking light cone (cheap additive cone mesh), EffectComposer with UnrealBloomPass (selective feel via emissive intensity), OutputPass, and FXAA or SMAA. Optional subtle SSAO/GTAO only if performance stays fine.
- Camera: OrbitControls with damping and sensible limits; smooth eased transitions for focus(); 'auto' is a slow cinematic orbit. A short intro dolly on load.
- Twin layout: place cell B beside cell A (offset in z or x) with floating CSS2D labels ("BASELINE — as delivered" / "REPAIRED — agent v2", text from setLayout labels); camera overview frames both.
- Performance: must hold ~60 fps on a laptop iGPU at 1080p with quality 'high'; 'low' disables bloom/shadows. Use instancing, share geometries/materials, dispose properly.
- Picking for panel controls: raycast clicks on buttons/E-stop/guard door → onControl callback (only when interactive). Show hover highlight + pointer cursor when interactive.

### Dev page
`web/scene-test.html` + `web/src/scene/dev.ts`: mounts the stage full-screen and plays a mock trace generated in dev.ts (motor on, boxes spawning and travelling, one picked, then a box overrunning and dropping, an E-stop press, guard opening, a stuck sensor), with keyboard shortcuts to switch layout and camera presets. Use it to verify. Verify visually: run the dev server in the background (`npm run dev -- --port 5174` inside web/) and take screenshots with the browser tools available to you (the built-in browser `mcp__Claude_Browser__*` tools: preview_start with a url like http://localhost:5174/scene-test.html, then computer screenshot). Iterate on the look until it is genuinely beautiful. Also make sure `npx tsc --noEmit` passes inside web/.

When done, reply with: the final API (confirm it matches), a short description of the scene, the list of assets with sources/licences, the commits you made, and any caveats.

